<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limbaži</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lemsal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Limbaži, German Lemsal, is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> region of northern Latvia, north-east of Riga. It grew up around a castle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, later archbishops, of Riga on lands once held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, and became one of the oldest towns in the country and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. It is now the centre of Limbaži Municipality.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limbazi.jpg" alt="Limbaži" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laima Gūtmane (simka) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limba%C5%BEu_vecpils%C4%93ta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Limbaži is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> region of northern Latvia, about 90 km north-east of Riga, with a population of 6,888. The name is believed to be a Latvianised form of the Livonian word Lembsel, meaning a wide isle in a forest swamp, and the German name Lemsahl comes from the same source. A folk etymology tells that in the 17th century a newly arrived Swedish minister heard the Latvian words limba and āži, meaning male goats, and took them for the name of the place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the site was a Livonian settlement called Lemisele, part of Metsepole. In the early 13th century Bishop Albert and the Teutonic knights destroyed the village while conquering Metsepole and built a castle, around which the new town of Lemsahl formed. Until the start of the 16th century seagoing vessels could reach it up the Svētupe, and ships from as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> came for honey, wax, lumber, grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. The town joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, held an annual conference of barons from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and at least three fairs a year, and served the Archbishops of Riga as a spring residence. Walled to protect both the archbishop and the market, it was second only to Riga in population.</p><p>By 1500 the Svētupe had become too shallow to navigate, trade moved elsewhere and a century of decline began. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> the forces of Ivan the Terrible burned the town in 1558, the Swedes burned it in 1567 and the Russians again in 1575. In 1602 Swedes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> fought over it and demolished its fortifications and walls, and when the wars ended only three houses and a handful of residents remained. Under Swedish rule it was renamed Limbaži. A fire destroyed the town again in 1747, after which its present layout began to develop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town grew again in the 19th century, from 674 inhabitants in 1821 to about 2,000 by 1900, helped by the hat-maker A. Tīls, who opened the felt company Limbažu filcs in 1876. A railway to Riga opened in 1934. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied the town from 4 July 1941 until 26 September 1944, and in the Soviet period it became a factory town with a farm machinery works, the felt factory and a large dairy. The remains of the old town wall, the ruins of the Archbishop's castle, the Livonian hill-fort at Ķezberkalns and St John's Lutheran Church survive, and the composer Kārlis Baumanis, who wrote the Latvian national anthem, is buried there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Limbaži” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Metsepole, whose name means 'forest land', was a district of the Finnic-speaking Livonians along the Gulf of Riga north of the Gauja, around modern <b>Limbaži</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/metsepole">Metsepole</a></li></ul></section>
